// Named points (RT-HOOK). A library marks a place where another task's
// event decides what it reads with Point(name); a test arms it with
// At(name, f), and the next task to reach the point runs f there, on its
// own task, before it goes on. So a law lands an event (an actor's commit,
// a server's answer) at that exact place on every run, where otherwise it
// would race for it. Only a test build has points: in a run build a Point
// or an At lowers to nothing (its arguments are evaluated when they are
// not literals), so a program holds no hook code. Go's testing/synctest
// and Loom-style schedulers are the prior art (runtime/v1 hook.go).

// At arms the point name: the next task that reaches Point(name) runs f on
// its own task, once, before it goes on. Points are the process's.
extern go ergo.local/prototype/runtime/v1.HookAt func At(name string, f own func())

// Point is the named point name: the calling task runs what a test armed
// there with At, and goes on. A point no test armed does nothing.
extern go ergo.local/prototype/runtime/v1.HookPoint func Point(name string)
